harrisburg is the capital of  pennsylvania and it's one of the brokest  cities in america the municipal  government has so much debt last year it  attempted to file for bankruptcy there  isn't much of a 1% to help Harrisburg  pay its debt a third of its residents  live below the poverty line and its  population has been gradually shrinking  Harrisburg has declined presents an  extreme case of what's happened in  municipalities across the country it's a  cautionary tale of how the ambitions of  a charismatic mayor can destroy a city  [Music]  American cities are drowning in debt  thanks to a spending binge that took off  in the mid 90s local governments owned  more than two point eight trillion  dollars and many lack the means to make  good on those obligations Harrisburg is  one of a handful of cities that have  effectively gone bankrupt and many more  are expected to follow suit reckless  spending did more than ruin Harrisburg's  balance sheet it undermined the city's  economy and purpose steven Russell Reed  who declined to be interviewed for this  story was mayor of the city for 28 years  and he bears most of the responsibility  for Harrisburg's nightmare if what  happened in Harrisburg happened to our  country we would clearly call it a  dictatorship Jason Smith is a designer  and small business owner who ran for  mayor against Reid in 2005 you can't  have an authentic public process when  money is moving around without oversight  and being spent on things that no one  wants I don't see anything wrong with  government being entrepreneurial Randi  King was mayor Reid's communications  director for 18 years we have to  understand the circumstance in the early  1980s Harrisburg was dead there was no  businesses downtown its population had  declined by more than half in the past  20 years  he believed differently about Harrisburg  and about cities in general he believed  that's a responsibility of the  leadership of the community to step in  and do things occasionally when there's  a need  sector isn't addressing many cities  spend taxpayer money subsidizing sports  arenas Harrisburg outre built a baseball  stadium and then it also bought the team  that plays there to prevent it from  moving to Springfield Massachusetts many  cities subsidize hotels and convention  centers Harrisburg outre bought a hotel  to keep it from shutting down many  cities subsidized museums and cultural  institutions mayor Reed wanted to build  his own museums which he believed would  transform Harrisburg into a tourist  mecca much as Washington DC and its many  museums draws millions of people to the  city each year or New York City  that's what Steve Reid's vision for  Harrisburg was read conceived of a  museum all that life on the frontier so  we headed west to start buying artifacts  he bought a stagecoach a Gatling gun  canned goods badges boots so you have  all that together in one town wow you  are a nationally scaled national class  tourism attraction but a warship from  the war of 1812 it was burned and sunk  but he got the embers anyway we always  used to joke you know sell Annie  Oakley's Underpants the museum plan was  ultimately shot down today most of  Reed's collection is sitting in a  warehouse it would have been a big hit  I'm really sorry that that never  manifested the mayor's dream of a  national civil war museum did become a  reality but in a way that would have  disappointed Honest Abe we'd spent more  than 16 million dollars in city money  acquiring the collection when that  wasn't enough he took money from the  Harrisburg Authority a branch of local  government that's responsible for  maintaining the city's waste in water  systems think he learned early on that  it's possible to borrow money for one  thing and move the money into funds no  one's heard of to get blank checks to  buy whatever you want  the mayor often lacked scruples when  spending those blank checks Harrisburg  borrowed 3.7 million dollars to help  build this office complex for a printing  company the project went awry when the  developer was indicted for embezzlement  Harrisburg's incinerator debacle was its  best-known and costliest venture the  incinerator which was built in the early  70s was a lemon from the very beginning  he hired an incompetent contractor to  renovate the plant and then neglected to  get insurance on the job today the city  owes 310 million dollars on the  incinerator that's a little more than  half Harrisburg's total liabilities  which add up to six hundred and ten  million according to the city controller  although nobody knows the exact figure  since Harrisburg is four years behind in  completing its financial statements  Harrisburg like many American cities was  so preoccupied with building white  elephants that it neglected core  functions like education and public  safety Harrisburg on a per capita basis  is one of the most violent cities in  Pennsylvania Matt bruit is the president  of the Commonwealth Foundation a  libertarian think-tank in Harrisburg  those who can live outside of the city  are certainly choosing to do so and  we've seen that with the population  shifts this is not a place to raise a  family about 60% of harrisburg students  are failing  according to Pennsylvania's statewide  achievement tests mayor Reed address  this problem by pouring money into  school construction the Harrisburg  school district is racked up two hundred  and seventy million dollars in debt  which is about two and a half times  Pennsylvania's average school debt per  capita everyone agrees the schools look  great they may not have books the  weightlifting room may not have weights  and the students might be the worst  performing in the state if not the  nation but the buildings are beautiful  failing schools were a major factor in  driving Harrisburg's middle class to the  suburbs  my family left Harrisburg two years ago  we decided we could go somewhere where  there's a good school district and  proper